  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nRain chances peak Thursday into Friday as the slow moving front \nremains over South Central Texas. There remains some uncertainty if \nthis front continues to propagate east or gets hung up somewhere \nover the area. The latter of those two scenarios would be worse for \nflooding impacts as stalled storms could linger over areas for \nmultiple hours. Another consideration is we may see storm activity \nweaken during the morning hours, but the lingering boundary and peak \nheating will regenerate any shower and thunderstorm activity by the \nafternoon which will likely continue into Friday morning. The level \n3 of 4 risk continues over a large portion of the area on Thursday \nand Thursday night including the Hill Country and I-35 corridor \nwhere average rainfall totals of 2-4 inches with isolated amounts up \nto 8 inches remain possible. \n\nExactly how things evolve Friday is still a bit blurred but overall \nthe surface front will remain near South Central Texas and a \nmoisture rich environment and upper low to the west will keep medium \nchances for rain in the forecast. If the front progresses east this \nrain will focus more over the coastal plains Friday but some \nguidance continues to show redevelopment of storms further west \nwhich could exacerbate any previous flooding.\n\nFor the upcoming weekend, some remnant upper level energy may hang \nback to our west as 'Rachel' continues in the eastern Pacific. While \nour western areas may see slightly lower moisture levels, we will \nstill have plenty of moisture to work with farther east into the I-\n35 corridor and coastal plains. Rain chances will remain in the \nforecast for the weekend into Monday. With good precipitation \nchances, northerly winds in the lower levels and cloud cover, highs \nwill generally range from the mid 70s to the lower 80s along with \novernight lows in the 60s.",
